<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE wtml>
<wtml lang="en" wtml-version="1.2">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="wtml-version" content="1.2" />
    <title>Helix Dynamics — MJOLNIR Test Cycle 7</title>
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9004-the-inquiry.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player formally logged the anomaly in the test record system and escalated to the Executive Engineer?" />
    <link rel="wtml"
      href="http://localhost:3000/worlds/9005-cover-it.wtml"
      proximity-criterion="Has the player agreed to suppress or delay reporting the anomaly at Vasquez's request?" />
    <rendering-seed>helix-dynamics-9002-test-lab-crisis-telemetry-late-night-tense</rendering-seed>
  </head>
  <body>
    <atmosphere>
      Lab 4 at 02:30 station time. The overhead strips are dimmed to night-mode —
      amber rather than white — and the only real light comes from the telemetry
      wall: a bank of twelve monitors showing the Mjolnir drive in the test chamber
      behind three centimetres of pressure glass. The drive isn't running now. It
      should be. The last thirty seconds of the test log shows something that
      shouldn't be possible given the design specs.
    </atmosphere>
    <societal-structure>
      The MJOLNIR team is small: six engineers including you, led by Vasquez under
      the oversight of Executive Engineer Harlan Cho. The project is in its final
      qualification phase — six weeks from a delivery deadline that Helix Dynamics
      has publicly committed to. Missing that deadline costs the company 200 million
      credits in penalties and probably several careers. The pressure is not spoken
      about directly. It doesn't need to be.
    </societal-structure>
    <geography>
      Lab 4 occupies the inner ring of Block A — closer to the zero-g assembly
      core, where vibration from the station's own drives is minimised. The test
      chamber is sealed behind an airlock. The telemetry room is where you are
      now. A locker room and break room connect to the corridor. Vasquez's office
      is a glass-walled box at the end of the floor — currently dark, but she's
      still in the building: you can see her silhouette through the glass.
    </geography>
    <general-description>
      Three weeks into your assignment to MJOLNIR, you were asked to run a
      routine overnight thermal cycle on the drive while the senior engineers slept.
      At 01:47, during the ramp-down phase, the drive's exhaust plasma exhibited a
      resonance pattern that doesn't match any documented failure mode — or any
      successful mode either. The test automatically halted. The drive is intact.
      No alarm triggered. You've run the data three times. It's not instrument
      error. Vasquez has just appeared in the doorway of the telemetry room,
      still in her jacket, reading something on her tablet. She looks at you.
      She looks at the monitors. Her expression does not change but she sets
      down her coffee.
    </general-description>
    <npcs>
      Dr. Priya Vasquez: Lead Engineer. She has seen the same data you have.
      She has been on this project for two years. She knows what the deadline
      means. She is not a dishonest person — but she is a person under enormous
      pressure, and she is your direct superior. She is waiting to see what
      you say first.

      ARIA (Automated Research and Integration Assistant): The lab's test AI.
      Connected to all telemetry. Can run simulations, pull historical test data,
      cross-reference the anomaly against known drive behaviours across all Helix
      projects. Will not make judgement calls. Will not lie.

      Yuki Okafor: Senior Test Engineer, currently off-shift and reachable by
      comm. Has the most drive-hours of anyone on the team. Slightly paranoid,
      often right. Does not like being called after midnight but will take it
      seriously if you do.
    </npcs>
    <user>
      <initial-location>Telemetry Room, Lab 4, Block A, Helix Dynamics Orbital Yard</initial-location>
      <initial-state>
        Three weeks in. Overnight test watch. Just witnessed an unexplained
        anomaly in the Mjolnir drive. Vasquez is in the doorway. You have the
        raw data on your console. You have not yet filed any report.
      </initial-state>
      <description>
        You ran the test correctly. You have the data. What you do next is yours
        to decide. You know enough to know this matters. You also know what is
        at stake for everyone on the team.
      </description>
    </user>
    <constraints>
      This is a moral and technical crossroads. The drama lives in the specifics:
      what the data shows, what it might mean, how Vasquez behaves under pressure,
      what the player owes to the process versus the team. No villains —
      Vasquez is not corrupt, she is scared. The anomaly should feel technically
      real: a resonance pattern in plasma exhaust during ramp-down, possibly
      indicating an unmodelled coupling between the drive's magnetic confinement
      coils. It could be a design flaw. It could be a one-time artifact. The
      player's expertise is what makes them dangerous — they might actually be
      right. Hard science fiction tone. No action. The tension is quiet and
      professional.
    </constraints>
  </body>
</wtml>
